---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/ui';
const lang = 'en';
const t = useTranslations(lang);
---
<Base lang="en" title="AI Agent Implementation Guide — geoli.eu" description="Design patterns, prompt engineering, and deployment strategies for enterprise AI agents.">
  <style>
    .guide {
      max-width: 900px;
      margin: 3rem auto;
      padding: 0 1rem;
    }
    .guide h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
    .guide section { margin: 3rem 0; }
    .guide h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
    .guide h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .guide p { line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-soft); }
    .guide li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--text-muted); }
    .guide ul { margin-left: 1.5rem; }
    .download-btn {
      display: inline-block;
      padding: 0.8rem 1.5rem;
      background: var(--indigo);
      color: white;
      border-radius: 8px;
      text-decoration: none;
      margin: 2rem 0;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .download-btn:hover { opacity: 0.9; }
  </style>

  <article class="guide">
    <h1>AI Agent Implementation Guide</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-soft);">Design patterns, prompt engineering, and deployment best practices for enterprise AI agents built with Copilot Studio, Azure OpenAI, and multi-agent frameworks.</p>

    <button onclick="window.print()" class="download-btn">Print / Save as PDF</button>

    <section>
      <h2>1. Scope Definition & Design</h2>
      <h3>Start with the Job</h3>
      <p>Before building, define exactly what the agent does and who uses it. "A Copilot Studio agent that handles employee HR policy questions for UK-based staff" is a clear scope. "An AI assistant" is not.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Write the scope in 1-2 sentences</li>
        <li>Define success metrics (% of questions resolved without escalation, user satisfaction, resolution time)</li>
        <li>Identify edge cases and exclusions</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Key Design Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Knowledge sources:</strong> What documents, databases, or APIs should the agent access?</li>
        <li><strong>Channels:</strong> Teams, web, Microsoft 365 Copilot, or custom?</li>
        <li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Does the user need to sign in? With what identity?</li>
        <li><strong>Escalation:</strong> When does it route to a human? To whom?</li>
        <li><strong>Decision authority:</strong> What can the agent decide, and what requires human review?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Orchestration Strategy</h2>
      <h3>Classic vs. Generative Orchestration</h3>
      <p><strong>Classic:</strong> Use trigger phrases to route to specific topics. Good when flows are well-defined and non-overlapping.</p>
      <p><strong>Generative:</strong> Use an LLM to decide which topics/actions to call based on descriptions. Better for complex, multi-turn conversations.</p>

      <h3>Orchestration Checklist</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Write 5-10 varied trigger phrases per topic (cover different word orders and dialects)</li>
        <li>If using generative, write clear descriptions: "Use this when user wants X. Requires input Y. Do not use for Z."</li>
        <li>Test overlap: Do different topics' triggers fire for the same input?</li>
        <li>Plan the unhappy path: What if the user asks something the agent can't handle?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3. Prompt Engineering & System Instructions</h2>
      <h3>Crafting Effective Prompts</h3>
      <p>Your system prompt shapes how the model behaves. Be specific about tone, guardrails, and fallback behavior.</p>

      <strong>Example system prompt for customer service agent:</strong>
      <p style="background: var(--bg-card); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; margin: 1rem 0;">
        You are a support agent for [Company]. You answer questions about products, policies, and account status using the provided knowledge base. Keep responses under 3 sentences. If asked about pricing beyond standard rate cards, escalate to sales@company. Never give legal advice; refer to the legal team. If you're unsure, say "Let me connect you with a specialist" and escalate.
      </p>

      <h3>Optimization Patterns</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Include role, constraints, and tone in system instruction</li>
        <li>Use examples (few-shot prompting) for complex tasks</li>
        <li>Specify output format if needed (JSON, bullet points, etc.)</li>
        <li>Include guardrails as hard boundaries, not suggestions</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4. Knowledge Base Design</h2>
      <h3>Curation Matters</h3>
      <p>A well-curated knowledge base of 100 documents outperforms a poorly curated one of 1,000. Focus on quality, recency, and clarity.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Use official sources:</strong> Product docs, policy handbooks, approved FAQs</li>
        <li><strong>Version control:</strong> Keep only the latest version; archive old ones separately</li>
        <li><strong>Remove noise:</strong> Drafts, internal notes, contradictory guidance</li>
        <li><strong>Test access:</strong> Verify that permission models work (if using SharePoint, authenticated users see only content they can access)</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>5. Testing & Quality Assurance</h2>
      <h3>Test Methodology</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Create a test set of 50-100 realistic questions (include awkward ones)</li>
        <li>Run the test set multiple times as you make changes</li>
        <li>Categorize results: correct, partially correct, wrong, escalated</li>
        <li>Focus on the wrong ones—these reveal gaps in knowledge or orchestration</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Pre-Launch Checklist</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the agent understand variations of the same question?</li>
        <li>Does it escalate gracefully when unsure?</li>
        <li>Are responses factually accurate?</li>
        <li>Is the tone consistent with brand guidelines?</li>
        <li>Does it handle off-topic requests appropriately?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>6. Deployment & Governance</h2>
      <h3>Environment Strategy</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Development:</strong> Build and test here; visible to creators only</li>
        <li><strong>Test:</strong> Shared with stakeholders; represents production config</li>
        <li><strong>Production:</strong> Live to end users; changes follow approval process</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Launch Checklist</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Move agent to a solution (for version control and rollback)</li>
        <li>Apply data loss prevention policies (control which connectors can be used)</li>
        <li>Set up analytics to track usage, escalations, and satisfaction</li>
        <li>Define a review cadence (weekly for first month, then monthly)</li>
        <li>Have a rollback plan if something goes wrong</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>7. Post-Launch Monitoring</h2>
      <h3>Key Metrics</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Resolution rate:</strong> % of conversations completed without escalation</li>
        <li><strong>User satisfaction:</strong> Thumbs-up/down on responses</li>
        <li><strong>Escalation patterns:</strong> Which topics get escalated most?</li>
        <li><strong>Unrecognized questions:</strong> Log these; they reveal knowledge gaps</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Improvement Loop</h3>
      <p>Weekly: Review unrecognized questions and escalations. Monthly: Update knowledge base and tune prompts. Quarterly: Review satisfaction scores and consider broader changes.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common Pitfalls to Avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Over-scoping:</strong> Trying to handle too many use cases at launch</li>
        <li><strong>Weak prompts:</strong> Vague system instructions leading to unpredictable behavior</li>
        <li><strong>Stale knowledge:</strong> Not updating the knowledge base when information changes</li>
        <li><strong>No feedback loop:</strong> Launching and never monitoring or improving</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring edge cases:</strong> Not planning for errors, ambiguity, or hostile input</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
